Razor doesn't correctly parse an escaped alias name in an `@using` directive. These are valid C# aliases, but Razor doesn't emit the alias declaration into the generated C#.

Found while investigating the review feedback on https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/pull/86243#discussion_r4406431152. This is a pre-existing compiler parsing limitation, separate from that PR's split-document rename fix.

### Repro

Put either of these examples in a `.razor` component.

An alias for a named type:

```razor
@using @class = System.String

@((@class)null)

@code {
    private @class? _value;
}
```

An alias for a tuple type:

```razor
@using @class = (int, int)

@(default(@class))

@code {
    private @class _value;
}
```

### Expected behaviour

Razor recognises the complete `@using` directive and emits the escaped alias into the generated C#, so its inline and member references bind to that alias.

### Actual behaviour

In the tuple repro, the generated implementation document contains an incomplete `using` inside `BuildRenderTree`, and ` = (int, int)` is emitted as markup. There is no alias declaration for the references to bind to. Both examples also return no rename edit when attempting to rename an existing `@class` alias, from either its declaration or an inline reference.

This was reproduced in shared cohost endpoint tests on the branch at `d02e7f3be877a6682c66d0e922e9e356f65ffd06`, before any escaped-identifier validation changes.

### Rename coverage when fixing this

Please include rename regression coverage alongside the parsing/code-generation tests. Fixing parsing alone isn't enough: the tooling's alias-identifier check currently uses `SyntaxFacts.IsValidIdentifier`, which rejects source spellings such as `@class`.

For example, start with:

```razor
@using Pair = (int, int)

@(default(Pair))

@code {
    private Pair _value;
}
```

Renaming `Pair` to `class` currently changes the references to `@class` but leaves `@using Pair = (int, int)` unchanged. The using-delta fallback excludes tuple aliases, so it doesn't recover the declaration edit.

Once escaped aliases parse correctly, cover renames both **to and from escaped names**, for named-type and tuple aliases. Verify the directive and all inline/member references are updated across both generated implementation and declaration documents. Include legacy `.cshtml` compatibility coverage too.

Version: roslyn-language-server 5.11.0 (dc1db3e7dee178e2744a73eb06dce107c07fa7b1). Also reproduced with Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Workspaces 5.0.0 alone (below). On main (6d8cf7de70d15d1f72a93471e3bbd5f2410717ed) the code below reads the same: `ProcessRegularFileChangesAsync` takes only the paths, and `TextLoader` still retries.

What happens
- Many source files of a loaded project are deleted on disk: a `git checkout` of another branch, a folder deleted or cleaned.
- The server's file watching reports each path. This happens through `workspace/didChangeWatchedFiles` (type 3, Deleted) or through its own watcher.
- `ProjectSystemProject.BatchingDocumentCollection.ProcessRegularFileChangesAsync` takes only the paths, whatever the change's kind, and gives each document a `WorkspaceFileTextLoader` for its file, which no longer exists.
- The project then reloads (`LoadedProject` matches the path against its globs), and the reload removes the documents.
- The project's next compilation is still built from the in-progress state, which first applies the pending text changes. Each load of a missing file goes through `TextLoader.LoadTextAsync`. That retries the IOException 5 times, `RetryDelay` (200 ms) apart, so it costs about 1 s for each deleted document, one after another.
- Meanwhile every request that needs the compilation waits: textDocument/diagnostic, documentHighlight, definition and so on. Completion and semantic tokens use frozen partial semantics and still answer.
- In a project of 1,000 small files with full-solution analysis on, the open file's next diagnostics pull answered:
  - about 13 s after 21 files were deleted;
  - about 4 minutes after the other 979 were deleted.
- The server's CPU was idle the whole time. Its EventPipe profile showed only waits (the retry's Task.Delay).

Repro (no LSP needed), net10.0 console app with Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Workspaces 5.0.0:

```csharp
using System.Collections.Immutable;
using System.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis;

int count = args.Length > 0 ? int.Parse(args[0]) : 100;
string folder = Directory.CreateTempSubdirectory("deleted-docs-").FullName;
ProjectId projectId = ProjectId.CreateNewId();
Solution solution = new AdhocWorkspace().CurrentSolution.AddProject(ProjectInfo.Create(projectId, VersionStamp.Create(), "P", "P",
    LanguageNames.CSharp, metadataReferences: [MetadataReference.CreateFromFile(typeof(object).Assembly.Location)]));
List<(DocumentId Id, string Path)> files = [];
for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
{
    string path = Path.Combine(folder, $"C{i}.cs");
    File.WriteAllText(path, $"static class C{i} {{ static void M() {{ int unused; }} }}");
    DocumentId id = DocumentId.CreateNewId(projectId);
    solution = solution.AddDocument(DocumentInfo.Create(id, Path.GetFileName(path), loader: new FileTextLoader(path, null), filePath: path));
    files.Add((id, path));
}
string kept = Path.Combine(folder, "Kept.cs");
File.WriteAllText(kept, "static class Kept { static int M() => 1; }");
DocumentId keptId = DocumentId.CreateNewId(projectId);
solution = solution.AddDocument(DocumentInfo.Create(keptId, "Kept.cs", loader: new FileTextLoader(kept, null), filePath: kept));
await solution.GetProject(projectId)!.GetCompilationAsync();

foreach ((_, string path) in files)
{
    File.Delete(path);
}
ImmutableArray<DocumentId> removed = [.. files.Select(f => f.Id)];

Stopwatch clock = Stopwatch.StartNew();
await solution.RemoveDocuments(removed).GetDocument(keptId)!.GetSemanticModelAsync();
Console.WriteLine($"{count} documents removed: Kept.cs's semantic model in {clock.Elapsed.TotalSeconds:0.00} s");

// What ProcessRegularFileChangesAsync does as each deletion is reported, then what the project's reload does.
Solution told = solution;
foreach ((DocumentId id, string path) in files)
{
    told = told.WithDocumentTextLoader(id, new FileTextLoader(path, null), PreservationMode.PreserveValue);
}
clock.Restart();
await told.RemoveDocuments(removed).GetDocument(keptId)!.GetSemanticModelAsync();
Console.WriteLine($"{count} documents told changed on disk, then removed: Kept.cs's semantic model in {clock.Elapsed.TotalSeconds:0.00} s");
Directory.Delete(folder, true);
```

Output (macOS, Apple Silicon):
```
21 documents removed: Kept.cs's semantic model in 0.01 s
21 documents told changed on disk, then removed: Kept.cs's semantic model in 21.25 s
100 documents removed: Kept.cs's semantic model in 0.01 s
100 documents told changed on disk, then removed: Kept.cs's semantic model in 101.10 s
```

Expected: deleting files never makes the remaining documents' answers wait on reads of the deleted ones. Any one of these would do it:
- A Deleted change doesn't swap the document's loader for one on a missing file: ProcessRegularFileChangesAsync takes the change's kind, or checks that the file exists.
- TextLoader doesn't retry FileNotFoundException or DirectoryNotFoundException.
- Removing a document drops its pending text change from the compilation tracker's in-progress state.

What our client does meanwhile: it tells the server of deleted files only once their projects have reloaded without them.
